'''Hodan''' (Burawi: ''Hodan''), officially the '''Hodan Administration''', is a [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|dependent urban administration]] in the [[Kankadadka Capital Region]] of [[Burawa]]. With a population of 612,345 as of 2024, it is the sixth-largest urban administration in the capital region. Located immediately south and west of [[Samara]], Hodan functions as a major university city and commuter suburb within the [[Kankadadka-Samara Megalopolis]]. 

Hodan is bordered by [[Saylac]] to the north, the [[Qaybin River]] to the east, and [[Samara]] to the south and west. The city is home to the northern campus of the [[People's University of Kankadadka]] and houses several offices of the [[Ministry of Labour (Burawa)|Ministry of Labour]]. Named after [[Hodan Kadar]], a socialist organizer and sister of [[Manaa Kadar]], the city was established in 1952 as a planned suburban development in the post-war expansion of the capital region.

== History ==

=== Planning and establishment ===

Hodan was established in 1948 as part of the post-war expansion of the [[Kankadadka Capital Region]]. Following the [[Great Adonian War]], the capital region experienced significant population growth as returning veterans, industrial workers, and government employees required housing. The Burawi government initiated a series of planned suburban developments to accommodate this growth, with Hodan designed as a commuter community to relieve pressure on central Samara.

The site selected for Hodan was agricultural land between the [[Qaybin River]] and the existing settlement of [[Saylac]]. The city was named in honour of [[Hodan Kadar]], a socialist organizer and sister of [[Manaa Kadar]], who had been instrumental in the labour movement of the early 20th century. The naming reflected the ruling Socialist Party's commitment to honouring figures from the movement's history.

=== Development and growth ===

The initial phase of development focused on residential construction to house the growing population of the capital region. The city grew rapidly through the 1950s, 1960s, and 1970s as working-age families settled in the area, drawn by employment opportunities in both Hodan and neighbouring Samara. In 1982, the northern campus of the [[People's University of Kankadadka]] was established, transforming Hodan into a university city and attracting students and faculty to the area.

In the 1980s, Hodan's population surpassed 400,000, necessitating expansion of infrastructure and public services. The city government invested in green spaces and parks, establishing a network of public recreational areas that remain a defining feature of the urban landscape.

=== Modern era ===

The 1990s saw significant transit investment with the construction of the [[Hodan Light Rail]] network, which opened in 1996. The light rail system, comprising six lines radiating from the central district, integrated Hodan more closely with the regional transportation network. In 2004, the extension of [[Samara Metro]] Line 14 into Hodan further enhanced connectivity.

== Geography ==

=== Location and boundaries ===

Hodan is situated in the southern portion of the [[Kankadadka Capital Region]], bordering [[Samara]] to the south and west, [[Saylac]] to the north, and the [[Qaybin River]] to the east. The Qaybin River forms a natural boundary between Hodan and areas to the east, and the river corridor provides green space and recreational opportunities.

The city's terrain is relatively flat, with gentle undulations typical of the coastal plain surrounding the capital region.

=== Districts ===

Hodan is divided into twelve administrative districts. [[District 1 (Hodan)|District 1]], the central district, serves as the civic and commercial heart of the city, containing the [[People's University of Kankadadka]] northern campus, the central rail station, and the primary municipal offices.

=== Climate ===

Hodan experiences a {{w|humid subtropical climate}} (Köppen: ''Cfa''), characteristic of the western coastal regions of Burawa along the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. The climate is moderated by oceanic influences, producing milder summers and more evenly distributed rainfall compared to the {{w|Mediterranean climate}} of the eastern coast.

Summer temperatures average 28–33°C, with occasional heat waves exceeding 38°C during July and August. Winter temperatures are mild, averaging 13–18°C, with frost rare but not unknown. The city experiences occasional cold snaps when polar air masses descend from the [[Great Kaftian Mountain Range]], with the record low of 4.0°C recorded in December.

Annual precipitation totals approximately 712 mm, distributed throughout the year with a winter maximum. The wettest months are December and January, while July and August are the driest. Humidity remains moderate year-round, averaging 68%.

{{Weather box Hodan}}

== Government and politics ==

As a dependent urban administration, Hodan falls under the jurisdiction of the [[Kankadadka Capital Region]] metropolitan administration. The city is governed by a mayor and municipal council responsible for local services, zoning, and infrastructure maintenance.

Several offices of the [[Ministry of Labour (Burawa)|Ministry of Labour]] are located in Hodan, making the city a secondary centre for labour administration within the capital region.

== Economy ==

Hodan's economy is closely integrated with that of [[Samara]] and the broader capital region. The city functions primarily as a commuter suburb, with a significant portion of its working population employed in Samara's government and service sectors.

The [[People's University of Kankadadka]] northern campus is a major economic anchor, employing faculty and staff while supporting businesses that serve the student population. Retail, food service, and rental housing constitute significant sectors of the local economy.

== Transportation ==

Hodan is a significant transit hub within the [[Kankadadka Capital Region]], with multiple rail and bus connections.

=== Rail ===

The [[Hodan Light Rail]] network, opened in 1996, comprises six lines radiating outward from the central district. The light rail system provides internal connectivity within Hodan and connects to regional rail services at the central station.

Hodan is connected to the [[Samara Metro]] system via [[Samara Metro Line 14|Line 14]], which terminates at [[Hodan Central Station]] in District 1. This connection provides direct access to central Samara and the broader metro network.

The city is also served by the [[Kankadadka Regional Rail System]], with Lines 2, 6, 11, 12, and 14 stopping at stations throughout Hodan. These regional lines connect Hodan to [[Kankadadka]], [[Galbeed]], and other urban administrations in the capital region.

=== Bus ===

Hodan operates an expansive bus network that complements the rail systems. The bus system provides coverage to areas not served by light rail and offers local circulation within each district.

== Education ==

=== Higher education ===

The [[People's University of Kankadadka]] maintains its northern campus in Hodan's District 1. The campus, established in 1956, houses several faculties and serves as a major centre for higher education in the capital region. The university's presence has shaped Hodan's character as a university city, with a significant student population and associated academic culture.

== Demographics ==

Hodan's population of 612,345 as of 2024 makes it the sixth-largest urban administration in the Kankadadka Capital Region. The city's demographic profile is characterised by working-age families, reflecting its development as a commuter suburb and university community.

The population includes a significant student contingent associated with the People's University of Kankadadka, though the majority of residents are permanent rather than transient.

== Culture and recreation ==

=== Green spaces ===

Hodan is known for its network of public parks and green spaces, a feature emphasised in the city's original planning. The [[Qaybin River]] corridor provides linear parkland along the eastern boundary, while neighbourhood parks are distributed throughout the twelve districts.

== Politics ==

Hodan is represented in the [[Parliament of Burawa]] by one senator in the [[Senate of Burawa]] and four representatives in the [[Lower House of Burawa]].

=== Senate ===

{{main|Senate of Burawa}}

Hodan holds one seat in the Senate, apportioned as a dependent urban administration within the [[Kankadadka Capital Region]]. The incumbent senator is '''[[Sagal Xuseen]]''' of the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]], elected in the 2024 Senate election.

=== Lower House ===

{{main|Lower House of Burawa}}

Hodan is divided into four legislative districts, each electing one representative to the Lower House. The current representatives are:

